# ██║  ██╗██████╔╝╚██████╔╝███████║
# ╚═╝  ╚═╝╚═════╝  ╚═════╝ ╚══════╝
# ---------------------------------
#   The console desktop's backgrounds: a picture made of characters.
#
# One `<name>.txt` per piece. `kdos background <name>` chooses one and
# `kdos background next` cycles; `~/.config/kdos/background.txt` is a person's
# own and outranks every one of these.
#
# A FILE IS WHAT A PROGRAM WOULD HAVE WRITTEN TO A TERMINAL. UTF-8 text with
# SGR colour in it, read by the same parser and the same render boundary every
# terminal window on this desktop goes through — so there is one answer to what
# `ESC [ 1 ; 36 m` means, and a piece written in cyan is drawn in whatever the
# accent's cyan-most slot is. `kdos theme` moves the art with everything else.
#
# COLOUR BY SGR AND NEVER BY A LITERAL. `ESC [ 3 1 m` through `ESC [ 3 7 m`,
# with `ESC [ 1 m` for the bright half, reduce to the eight slots by nearest
# distance. A 24-bit colour is accepted and reduces the same way, but it is
# reducing a colour that was already chosen against some other palette, so it
# lands somewhere nobody picked.
#
# NOTHING BUT TEXT AND SGR. No cursor motion, no clear, no scroll region: the
# piece is measured by counting cells and lines, and a sequence that moved the
# cursor would draw somewhere the measure does not know about.
#
# THE GLYPHS ARE THE ONES THE CONSOLE FONT HAS, AND THAT IS A HARD LIMIT ON
# tty1. The kernel's text plane carries 512 glyphs; a character outside them
# renders BLANK there while the same file is correct in `kdos-term` and over
# `ssh`, so a piece using more is a piece that looks like a different picture
# on the screen it was made for. The three shipped pieces use only:
#
#   ─ │ ┌ ┐ └ ┘ ├ ┤ ┬ ┴ ┼        the whole single box set
#   ═ ║ ╔ ╗ ╚ ╝ ╬                the double set the font HAS
#   █ ▒ ░                        three shades, which is all there are
#   · ° ∙                        and ASCII
#
# WHAT IS NOT THERE, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: ╠ ╣ ╦ ╩ (the double tees),
# ▓ (the fourth shade), and every half and eighth block — ▀ ▄ ▌ ▐ and the rest.
# The font is `ter-kdos32n`, which is Terminus' `xos4-2` charset with six
# spacing diacritics swapped for the double box glyphs the block logo needs, so
# the list above is that file and not a guess. `selftest.sh` reads the same
# charset out of the port and fails a piece that uses anything else.
#
# CP437 ART IS CONVERTED WHEN IT IS PACKAGED, never at runtime: a decoder on
# the draw path would be a second character-set decision beside the terminal's.
#
# The piece is drawn CENTRED behind the icons. A BBS screen was eighty by
# twenty-five and a display is not, so a piece anchored to a corner would sit
# in one on every screen bigger than the one it was drawn for.
# ---------------------------------
